# oregrade — Claude Code working brief

Scores whether an open source project can support a company. Not a health score:
health tools measure whether a project is well-run, this measures whether it is
commercially takeable. Cost-ordered gates short-circuit, survivors get scored.

Default mode is `fund` (underwriting a company that exists), not `form` (creating
one). That was set on evidence — see `audience_modes.default_rationale` in the
rubric. Do not change it.

---

## THE ONE HARD RULE

**Nothing in `src/` may read `benchmarks/outcomes.yaml`.** Ever. Not to check a
result, not to "validate," not in a test fixture.

The project's only real claim is that the rubric was built without knowledge of
outcomes and then tested against them. If scoring code sees outcomes, thresholds
get tuned toward them — not deliberately, just by iterating until the numbers
look good. That is p-hacking, and it makes the published result worthless.

Enforced structurally, not by intention:

```
benchmarks/
  projects.yaml     repo, fund_snapshot, surface, entity_at_snapshot   ← src/ MAY read
  outcomes.yaml     repo, label, resolution                            ← src/ MAY NOT read
src/                scoring. imports projects.yaml only.
evaluate.py         top level. the ONLY file that opens outcomes.yaml.
runs/run-001.json   scores, committed BEFORE evaluate.py is written
```

Add a test that fails if any file under `src/` contains the string `outcomes`.
Split `labels.yaml` into those two files as step one.

Order of work: build scorer → run on all 25 → **commit `runs/run-001.json`** →
only then write `evaluate.py`. If run-001 separates the classes poorly, that is
the finding. Record it and do not retune. Any later tuning pass gets its own run
file and a one-line note saying what changed and why.

